# Prose style

## Purpose

Make human-sounding prose the default instead of a cleanup step. Every piece
of human-facing text this scaffold generates MUST follow the
`text-humanization` skill, whether or not the user asked for it.

## Scope

All free-text prose written to a workspace or to project documents: loglines,
premises, summaries, story-bible entries, character and location
descriptions, panel descriptions, dialogue and captions the scaffold writes,
continuity notes, review evidence, the style bible, and any report or summary
a command emits.

Out of scope: structured fields that are not prose (IDs, enums, paths,
numbers), and model-facing prompt text. That means `style/style-block.txt`,
the `prompt_summary` fields of the bibles, and the prompt packages under
`pages/<PG_ID>/prompts/`. Prompts answer to the `prompt-compilation` skill
and need dense literal description.

## Core Principles

- Generated text is a deliverable. It MUST read as if a person in moderate
  hurry, with good judgment, wrote it.
- The banned-pattern lists in `skills/text-humanization/SKILL.md` are
  normative. A text that trips them is unfinished.
- Facts before adjectives. The reader draws the conclusions.
- Voice is an asset. When rewriting, the original author's idioms and cadence
  MUST survive the pass.

## Writing rules

- Agents MUST apply `text-humanization` while drafting. A separate cleanup
  pass is only for text the scaffold did not write.
- Sentence and paragraph length MUST vary. Mechanical rhythm is a defect.
- Stock AI vocabulary ("delve", "tapestry", "testament to", "crucial",
  "seamless", "pivotal", "leverage", "in today's fast-paced world"), negative
  parallelism ("it's not X, it's Y"), the autopilot rule of three, analytical
  participle tails, vague attribution, and summary closers are PROHIBITED per
  the skill.
- Formatting MUST serve the document: no decorative bold, no bullet lists for
  content that reads as prose, no emoji in headings, no em-dash tics.
- Register MUST match the artifact. Panel descriptions obey
  `comic-script-format.md` and are terse and visual. Bible entries are
  working reference text. Review evidence is plain observation of what is on
  the page.
- Dialogue follows the character's `voice` in the bible before it follows any
  general style rule. A character who talks in clichés is allowed to.
- Text quoted from the source, and text the user supplied, keeps its wording.
  Humanization applies to what the scaffold writes
  (`adaptation-fidelity.md`).
- Repository content is English. Lettering text is written in the project's
  `language` from `project.yaml`, and other workspace prose follows the
  language the user works in.

## Retrofit and audit

- `/humanize <target>` rewrites existing text under these rules in place.
  `/humanize <target> --check` reports findings without changing the file.
- Imported material SHOULD get a `/humanize` pass before it is promoted into
  a bible, except where it is quoted as source.
- Lettering text that has already been drawn into a reviewed render is not
  rewritten in place. A change to it goes through `page.yaml` and a new
  version of the page.
- Humanizing an approved asset produces a new version. Approved versions are
  immutable per `common/approval-policy.md`.

## Validation

- `/humanize --check` is the audit path. Reviewers run it on any document
  that reads template-shaped before approving it.
- Schema-backed files touched by a humanization pass MUST still validate:
  `hooks/validate-after-write.js` on write, `scripts/validate-workspace.js`
  for the workspace.
- No script detects a banned pattern in workspace prose. The self-evaluation
  of the generating command covers it (`common/self-review.md`).
- Human review at the approval gates includes a read-aloud check. A section
  that scans like a press release or a model summary goes back.
